<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atatürk's reforms</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kemalist reforms</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Atatürk's reforms were the political, legal, cultural and social changes carried out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a> between 1923 and 1938 to build a secular, national and modern state in place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. They included the abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> in 1924, the closure of religious courts and orders, a civil code based on Swiss law, the adoption of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet in 1928 and the vote for women in national elections in 1934. Their guiding principles, known as Kemalism, were written into the constitution in 1937.</p></div>
